{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794667091,"nonce":"caVnGz6Zs69XZm2N7liMYcsYyzA=","message":"Fixed for the engine, desktop numbers below; the Pixel run is pending (the phone dropped off adb before the last run).\n\nCause: each faded card with a label became its own offscreen group. A group costs a clear, a draw, an alpha pass and a blit, each its own render pass and submit, so a page of N cards took about 5N passes a frame. A CPU sample of 60 cards on an M1 Pro put most of the main thread in wgpu pass end, submit and encoder creation.\n\nFix: consecutive plain opacity groups (no effect, blend or transform; primitives only, inside the viewport, at most 512 px a side) are drawn into one atlas and composited in one pass, in order (renderer/overlay/layer_batch.rs). The pixels match the one-at-a-time path within 1/255 for rectangles and 2/255 for labelled cards (tests in blinc_gpu and blinc_app).\n\nDesktop, css_window_check, text cards with opacity and scale, CPU as the slope of ps cputime over 18 s:\n60 cards: before 0.92 to 0.96 cores, about 22 fps, paint median 22 to 23 ms; after 0.25 to 0.27 cores, 63 to 67 fps, 3.8 to 4.0 ms.\n200 cards: before 0.84 to 0.95 cores, 18 fps, 27 ms; after 0.39 to 0.40 cores, 39 fps, 7.3 to 7.5 ms.\n\nNot yet measured on a phone: the iPhone memory at 100 and 200 cards, and the Pixel (before: 9 fps at 60 cards, 2 fps at 200).","files":null}]}